  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 305 AM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nCurrent surface observations show breezy winds (30-40 mph) across \nthe Front Range mountains due to increased flow aloft. Expect breezy \nwinds to continue throughout the morning, particularly off the \nCheyenne Ridge. With a slight decrease in moisture due to the \nwest/northwest flow aloft, shower and thunderstorm coverage may be \nmore limited today. However, CAMs still favor convection\ninitiating around 1/2 PM across the Front Range mountains, and \ntracking east onto the I-25 Corridor and adjacent plains. Model \nSkew-T soundings favor the inverted-v profile with DCAPE values \nreaching up to 1200- 1400 J/kg. This means any high-based storm \nthat does develop is capable of producing strong outflow winds. \nMost gusts should stay below severe limits (40-55 mph), but can't \nrule out a few 60-65 mph gusts. \n\nIn addition, there is a conditional severe weather threat for the \nfar northeastern plains later in the afternoon/evening. CAMs favor \nthunderstorms to develop along a dryline in the wake of a surface \ntrough. Models continue to disagree where the dryline sets up, \nbut if it can recede into the far northeastern plains, a few \nsupercells could impact that area. With better shear/instability \nprofiles, hail and strong winds will be the main threats. \n\nOn Tuesday, a weak cold front will move through the area, which will \nbring more seasonable temperatures. Forecasted highs should get up \nto the high 80s across the plains. Another round of scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms expected to develop in the afternoon. \nThe severe threat does not look that impressive, as overall \ninstability looks limited compared to farther east in Nebraska and\nKansas. However, these high-based storms still could produce \nstrong outflow winds, as DCAPE values should get up to 1200-1300 \nJ/kg. \n\nThe \"cooler\" temperatures should last through Wednesday, with highs \nreaching up to the mid-to-high 80s across the plains. Afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms expected again for the high terrain and \nplains. Modeled soundings show more favorable shear profiles, which \nwould support a few strong/severe storms for the I-25 Corridor and \nadjacent plains. \n\nFor the later half of the week, ensemble guidance favors heat \nrebuilding over the area, with temperatures reaching back up the mid-\n90s by Friday and Saturday. The upper level ridge shoddily slide \nback west, which will start cutting off our moisture coming into \nthe region. However, there should still be enough moisture to \nsupport isolated-to-scattered afternoon thunderstorms. By the end \nof the weekend, guidance does hint at the upper level ridge \nweakening and shifting east due to a shortwave trough entering \nthe Pacific northwest. With west/southwest flow aloft, moisture \nshould begin filtering in again, with increase in shower and \nthunderstorm activity towards the beginning of next week.",
